## Case 1: The Vulnerable DOM-XSS Source Structure
In this configuration, the backend server safely serves a static file. However, the client-side JavaScript reads the query string and directly uses innerHTML or pushes it straight to a location sink. This is exactly what your script maps when running its multi-vector fuzzing routines. [1, 2] 

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>DOM-Based XSS Target Lab</title>
</head>
<body>

    <div id="wrapper">
        <h1>Search Results</h1>
        <!-- The user string is dynamically injected into this node container -->
        <p id="search-output">You searched for: <span id="query-string"></span></p>
    </div>

    <script>
        // Source: Grab the query string parameters from the window location bar
        const urlParams = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);
        const searchInput = urlParams.get('search');

        if (searchInput) {
            // DANGEROUS SINK: innerHTML evaluates string data as live HTML tags 
            // If searchInput is "<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>", it triggers instantly.
            document.getElementById('query-string').innerHTML = searchInput;

            // ALTERNATIVE EXECUTION SINK: Explicit Pseudo-Protocol Routing Handler
            // If searchInput is "javascript:alert(1)", clicking this link executes code.
            const actionLink = document.createElement('a');
            actionLink.href = searchInput; 
            actionLink.innerText = "Click here to process search trace";
            document.getElementById('wrapper').appendChild(actionLink);
        }
    </script>
</body>
</html>

