Would you like me to show you how to write the fixed, completely safe JavaScript functions for these templates to ensure inputs are processed securely without rendering code?


No, when this happens in a browser framework like Vue or AngularJS, it is not Remote Code Execution (RCE). It is Client-Side Template Injection (CSTI), which behaves exactly like Cross-Site Scripting (XSS).
Because the code executes strictly inside the victim's web browser, it can steal session data or manipulate the page layout, but it cannot access or damage the backend server.
However, if the web application uses a Server-Side Template Engine (like Thymeleaf, Freemarker, Jinja2, or EJS) and evaluates that exact same ${alert(1)} string on the server backend before sending the HTML to the user, then it becomes Server-Side Template Injection (SSTI), which can lead to full RCE. [1] 
------------------------------
## The Two Faces of Template Injection
Whether this payload is a simple browser glitch or a catastrophic server takeover depends entirely on where the template code is being processed.

| Feature | Client-Side (CSTI) | Server-Side (SSTI) |
|---|---|---|
| Where Code Runs | Inside the user's web browser. | Inside the backend web server. |
| Impact Level | XSS Equivalent (Steals cookies, session hijacking). | Full RCE (Full server control, database access). |
| Vulnerable Tech | Vue.js, Angular, AngularJS, React. | Jinja2 (Python), Twig (PHP), EJS (Node), Thymeleaf (Java). |
| Attacker Payload | Looks for browser functions: ${alert(1)} | Looks for OS commands: ${run_command('id')} |

------------------------------
## How it Escalates to RCE on a Backend Server (SSTI Example)
If the target web server is built with Node.js and uses a server-side template engine improperly, an attacker can modify the payload to pivot from a simple browser pop-up to executing actual terminal commands on the host machine.
## The Vulnerable Backend Code (Node.js / Express / EJS Example)

// DANGEROUS: Concatenating user input directly into a server-side template template string
app.get('/search', (req, res) => {
    const searchInput = req.query.search; 
    
    // The server compiles the user string as active backend template logic!
    const htmlTemplate = `<div>You searched for: ${searchInput}</div>`;
    res.render('search_page', { template: htmlTemplate });
});

## The Weaponized RCE Payload
Instead of sending a client-side instruction like alert(1), the attacker switches the payload to access backend process environments:

?search=${process.mainModule.require('child_process').execSync('id')}

When the server processes this request, it doesn't show anything to the user—instead, it silently executes the Linux id or whoami commands directly inside your Ubuntu target terminal environment, granting the attacker a backdoor into the infrastructure.
